;; Dex Capability Exchange — Claude Code deep adapter containment profile
;; (gates.md G1). Applied by MacOSStrategy via:
;;   sandbox-exec -D PY=<python> -f claude_code_containment.sb <python> -m ...
;;
;; The inspection process may read, and nothing else:
;;   - no network (deny system-socket, deny network*): the process cannot
;;     reach the network; Linux denies socket creation outright, while
;;     GitHub's macos-14 Seatbelt runners deny outbound use at connect time;
;;   - no file writes (deny file-write*): the target and everything else
;;     stays byte-identical; results leave on the inherited stdout pipe;
;;   - no process-exec except the Python interpreter itself (the sandbox
;;     must exec it once to start): no shell spawning.
;;
;; The child process additionally proves these denials at runtime before
;; any target read (contained.py prove_containment) and refuses to collect
;; if any probe shows a capability — the profile and the proof fail closed
;; together.

(version 1)
(allow default)

;; `network*` (network-outbound, network-inbound, network-bind) is checked at
;; connect/sendto/bind time, so under it a buggy collector may still get a
;; live fd from socket(2). The socket(2) call itself is a separate Seatbelt
;; operation — `system-socket` — which is why every deny-default Apple profile
;; that does ordinary TCP has to allow both (e.g.
;; com.apple.security.XPCAcmeService.sb, com.apple.securityuploadd.sb and
;; webdav_agent.sb each pair `(allow network-outbound)` with
;; `(allow system-socket)`). The deny stays here as the narrowest known
;; expression of "no socket creation", but CI on GitHub macos-14 has disproved
;; the stronger claim that it blocks Python AF_INET/AF_INET6 socket creation
;; there. On those runners the honest proof is `connect-denied`: no egress,
;; with a live-but-unusable fd.
;;
;; `network*` stays as a second, independent layer: if a future macOS
;; stopped enforcing system-socket for a domain, connect would still be
;; denied, and prove_containment still raises rather than collects if a
;; connection ever actually succeeds.
(deny system-socket)
(deny network*)
(deny file-write*)

;; An enumerated exec set, never a subpath: PY is sys.executable, PYREAL its
;; realpath, and PYAPP the framework launcher a python.org build re-execs
;; during start-up (Resources/Python.app/Contents/MacOS/Python). A subpath
;; over the install prefix would admit whatever later appears under it; three
;; literals admit exactly three binaries, none of them a shell.
(deny process-exec*)
(allow process-exec (literal (param "PY")))
(allow process-exec (literal (param "PYREAL")))
(allow process-exec (literal (param "PYAPP")))
